// A binding marked `errno` answers a `Result` whose error is an `Errno` or an integer, over a C
// function answering an integer, -1 where it fails; its `Ok` holds the C result, kept as an integer or
// left out, and the out values, never a status of its own.
// error: the result of the C binding `plain` is `int` in Avin and `int` in C: a binding marked `errno` answers a `Result`: `Ok` of what C answers where it does not answer -1, and `Err` of why where it does, an `Errno` or an integer, as in `Result[int, Errno]`
// error: the result of the C binding `worded` is `Result[int, string]` in Avin and `int` in C: a binding marked `errno` answers a `Result`: `Ok` of what C answers where it does not answer -1, and `Err` of why where it does, an `Errno` or an integer, as in `Result[int, Errno]`
// error: the result of the C binding `text` is `Result[string, Errno]` in Avin and `const char *` in C: `errno` marks a C function that answers -1 where it fails, so its result is a C integer
// error: the result of the C binding `status` is `Result[Result[unit, int], Errno]` in Avin and `int` in C: the `Ok` of a binding marked `errno` holds what C answers, which is no status of its own: C answers -1 where it fails
// error: the result of the C binding `truth` is `Result[bool, Errno]` in Avin and `int` in C: the C result of a binding marked `errno` is kept as an integer, or left out

import avin.c.Errno

@c("int f(int x)", errno)
plain(x: int) -> int

@c("int f(int x)", errno)
worded(x: int) -> Result[int, string]

@c("const char *g(int x)", errno)
text(x: int) -> Result[string, Errno]

@c("int f(int x)", errno)
status(x: int) -> Result[Result[unit, int], Errno]

@c("int f(int x)", errno)
truth(x: int) -> Result[bool, Errno]

main() = 0
